## Refactored with Async/Await

Here's the refactored version with several improvements:

```javascript
async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await db.query('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);

  if (!rows.length) {
    throw new Error(`User with id "${id}" not found`);
  }

  return rows[0];
}
```

### Usage Example

```javascript
// Basic usage
try {
  const user = await getUser(1);
  console.log(user);
} catch (err) {
  console.error('Failed to get user:', err.message);
}

// Or with .then()/.catch() if not in an async context
getUser(1)
  .then(user => console.log(user))
  .catch(err => console.error(err.message));
```

---

### What Changed and Why

| Before | After | Reason |
|---|---|---|
| `callback(err)` | `throw new Error(...)` | Async functions reject the returned Promise on throw |
| `callback(null, rows[0])` | `return rows[0]` | Return value becomes the resolved Promise value |
| Error handling scattered | Centralized in `try/catch` | Easier to read and maintain |
| Callback parameter required | No extra parameter needed | Cleaner function signature |

---

### If `db.query` Doesn't Support Promises

If your database driver is **callback-only** (e.g., older versions of `mysql`), wrap it first using `util.promisify`:

```javascript
const { promisify } = require('util');
const dbQuery = promisify(db.query).bind(db);

async function getUser(id) {
  const rows = await dbQuery('SELECT * FROM users WHERE id = ?', [id]);

  if (!rows.length) {
    throw new Error(`User with id "${id}" not found`);
  }

  return rows[0];
}
```

> **Note:** Modern database libraries like `mysql2`, `pg`, and `mongoose` support Promises natively, so `promisify` is usually unnecessary.
